4. Their Fig. 4, done better: aggregate instead of anecdote.

Their version is one episode. Yours should be thrust magnitude vs distance-to-riser, mean ± IQR over 100+ episodes, with the riser at x = 0. If the policy really learned "thrust only when needed," that plot shows a sharp band that rises at the step and falls after — across the whole distribution, not one lucky run. Add servo angle as a second trace; their figure omits the servo, which is the actuator that makes this robot distinctive.

5. RL vs PID on the same riser, overlaid.

Same four panels, two controllers, one plot each. Shows how they differ — the PID holds near-constant thrust (your baseline's T_hold), the policy modulates. That's exactly the IROS'26 hardware finding (1348–1355 µs constant vs 1145–1435 µs modulated) reproduced in sim where you can run it 100 times instead of 3.

